---

![Colorful Unicode character map](cover.jpg)

Image: [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unicode_character_map_(color).jpg)

For example, if we have the following sentence: `¿Hola cómo estás?`,
transform it into `Hola como esta`.

```elixir
nombre =
"¿Hola cómo estás?"
|> String.trim()

# We standardize the characters to their canonical unicode representation
|> :unicode.characters_to_nfd_binary()

# We clean any unicode character that is not a space
|> String.replace(~r/\p{Mn}/u, "")
```

We can also use the `String.normalize/1` function instead of
`:unicode.characters_to_nfd_binary/1`.

```elixir
String.normalize(:nfd)
```

Now we can remove the characters that are not valid for a name. We
get the characters that are not letters and store them in a unique
list.

```elixir
noletras = nombre
|> String.replace(~r/0-9a-zA-Z/, "")
|> String.graphemes()
|> Enum.uniq()
```

Finally we replace the unwanted characters.

```elixir
nombre = String.replace(nombre, noletras, "")
```

- <https://www.erlang.org/doc/apps/stdlib/unicode.html#characters_to_nfd_binary/1>
- <https://unicode.org/reports/tr15/#Norm_Forms>
